WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:22.860
Robert Louis Stevenson: Living Life Through
Imagination

00:00:22.860 --> 00:00:27.920
Robert Louis Stevenson holds a special place
in the hearts and minds of many readers.

00:00:27.920 --> 00:00:31.949
He often served as an introduction to real,
grown-up books.

00:00:31.949 --> 00:00:35.870
Stories like Kidnapped and Treasure Island
may be considered classics, but they're also

00:00:35.870 --> 00:00:40.290
full of adventure and intrigue — the sort
of stories that make you wish you were one

00:00:40.290 --> 00:00:45.329
of his characters, sailing the high seas,
looking for buried treasure, fighting to reclaim

00:00:45.329 --> 00:00:46.329
your legacy.

00:00:46.329 --> 00:00:51.059
His stories are undoubtedly all classic works
of literature, and while many are told against

00:00:51.059 --> 00:00:57.489
the backdrop of very real events, like the
Jacobite rising of 1745, there's also something

00:00:57.489 --> 00:01:02.920
about them that speaks to a sense of grand
imagination, a longing for adventure, and

00:01:02.920 --> 00:01:04.129
a deep sense of justice.

00:01:04.129 --> 00:01:10.250
It’s time to pull out your spyglass and
take a peek at the life of this 19th century

00:01:10.250 --> 00:01:11.600
Scottish writer.

00:01:11.600 --> 00:01:16.720
You'll find there's a reason he was so good
at telling some of the tallest tales imaginable,

00:01:16.720 --> 00:01:19.190
setting them against epic, historical backdrops.

00:01:19.190 --> 00:01:26.210
For much of his life, his world was only what
he could see -- or dream -- from his sickbed.

00:01:26.210 --> 00:01:32.060
An ill child who lived through imagination

00:01:32.060 --> 00:01:35.729
Childhood often lays the groundwork for a
lifetime, and for Robert Louis Stevenson,

00:01:35.729 --> 00:01:38.049
that was definitely the case.

00:01:38.049 --> 00:01:43.070
He was born on November 13, 1850, the only
child of middle-class parents.

00:01:43.070 --> 00:01:47.490
His mother came from a family of high education,
growing up in a world of church ministers

00:01:47.490 --> 00:01:48.510
and lawyers.

00:01:48.510 --> 00:01:53.439
His father had a job that most people would
consider not only respectable, but pretty

00:01:53.439 --> 00:01:58.320
cool: he was an engineer, and he was involved
in building numerous deep-sea lighthouses

00:01:58.320 --> 00:01:59.969
along Scotland's coast.

00:01:59.969 --> 00:02:05.829
For much of his childhood, the family lived
at 17 Heriot Row in Edinburgh, and it's a

00:02:05.829 --> 00:02:10.750
place anyone with a healthy respect for Georgian
architecture would truly appreciate.

00:02:10.750 --> 00:02:15.890
Built some time between 1802 and 1806, it
was still fairly new when the Stevenson's

00:02:15.890 --> 00:02:20.830
only child roamed the halls; it's easy to
imagine a young boy running up and down the

00:02:20.830 --> 00:02:25.530
stairs, jousting his way down the hallways,
staging adventures in the garden, perhaps

00:02:25.530 --> 00:02:31.330
turning the giant enamel bath his father had
installed into a swath of ocean filled with

00:02:31.330 --> 00:02:33.070
monstrous creatures.

00:02:33.070 --> 00:02:36.180
Unfortunately, Stevenson's childhood was very
different.

00:02:36.180 --> 00:02:40.471
When his parents welcomed him into the world,
he was a perfectly normal, healthy boy, but

00:02:40.471 --> 00:02:45.070
it wasn't long before he started suffering
from severe respiratory problems.

00:02:45.070 --> 00:02:49.590
Those problems would shape not only his childhood,
but the rest of his life.

00:02:49.590 --> 00:02:54.590
Stevenson spent a lot of time during his formative
years fussed over by anxious parents and cared

00:02:54.590 --> 00:02:58.830
for by a nurse who would end up having a major
impact on his adult life.

00:02:58.830 --> 00:03:03.470
He wasn't — as one might expect — hunting
for buried treasure in the back garden, or

00:03:03.470 --> 00:03:07.250
pretending to ride imaginary horses up and
down the streets of the city.

00:03:07.250 --> 00:03:12.130
Instead, he spent much of his time swathed
in blankets and shawls, doted on by the adults

00:03:12.130 --> 00:03:15.130
around him, learning that he was very fragile.

00:03:15.130 --> 00:03:18.780
Historians never quite decided just what it
was he suffered from.

00:03:18.780 --> 00:03:23.210
The original diagnosis was tuberculosis, but
today, it's suspected that modern medicine

00:03:23.210 --> 00:03:28.550
would come up with a diagnosis of bronchiectasis,
which is a series of continuous infections

00:03:28.550 --> 00:03:32.880
resulting from damage done to the lining of
the bronchial tubes — a condition often

00:03:32.880 --> 00:03:37.550
said to be made worse by the presence of dust
and air particles, and often starting with

00:03:37.550 --> 00:03:38.810
a childhood illness.

00:03:38.810 --> 00:03:43.480
If that's the case, there's a sad addendum
to his sickly childhood: his well-meaning

00:03:43.480 --> 00:03:48.630
caregivers were always afraid of drafts making
him ill, so the windows in the home were always

00:03:48.630 --> 00:03:49.630
closed.

00:03:49.630 --> 00:03:51.110
Would some fresh air have made a difference?

00:03:51.110 --> 00:03:53.100
It's impossible to say.

00:03:53.100 --> 00:03:57.150
What we do know is that Stevenson's childhood
nurse had a profound impact on him.

00:03:57.150 --> 00:04:01.110
Her name was Alison Cunningham, but everyone
called her Cummy, and she lived with the household

00:04:01.110 --> 00:04:02.830
for a full two decades.

00:04:02.830 --> 00:04:07.120
By all accounts, she loved the young Stevenson
as if he were her own son, and in addition

00:04:07.120 --> 00:04:10.080
to being a caregiver, she was also a story-teller.

00:04:10.080 --> 00:04:14.450
True, Stevenson was often confined to his
home, or to the strictly regimented life of

00:04:14.450 --> 00:04:17.500
a health spa, but only in the physical sense.

00:04:17.500 --> 00:04:20.910
Cummy entertained her young charge with a
variety of tales.

00:04:20.910 --> 00:04:25.780
Her firm Calvinist beliefs formed the backbone
of many of the stories she regaled him with,

00:04:25.780 --> 00:04:31.080
but she also told tales of ghosts, ghouls,
and other supernatural spectres.

00:04:31.080 --> 00:04:33.220
Nights were particularly difficult for Stevenson.

00:04:33.220 --> 00:04:37.810
From a young age, he suffered from insomnia,
and was was often kept awake for all hours

00:04:37.810 --> 00:04:40.210
by illness and feverish vision.

00:04:40.210 --> 00:04:44.380
Frequently be lulled back to sleep by his
father, who would sit by his side and invent

00:04:44.380 --> 00:04:46.390
conversations with imaginary people.

00:04:46.390 --> 00:04:51.470
The conversation was always droll but always
soothing, sending the young boy drifting off

00:04:51.470 --> 00:04:56.630
to sleep, escorted by made-up characters and
conversations plucked from thin air.

00:04:56.630 --> 00:05:01.570
And sometimes, it was Cummy who filled Stevenson's
sleepless nights with stories, fueling his

00:05:01.570 --> 00:05:02.920
nighttime delirium.

00:05:02.920 --> 00:05:07.210
If you look at Stevenson’s childhood through
a more modern lens on parenting, and it's

00:05:07.210 --> 00:05:11.050
easy to put some sort of blame on the shoulders
of his guardians.

00:05:11.050 --> 00:05:14.990
He was also susceptible to nightmares, and
there's one school of thought that suggests

00:05:14.990 --> 00:05:20.300
the mix of Cummy's religious doctrine, ghost
stories, and the fevers he frequently ran

00:05:20.300 --> 00:05:23.270
fueled those nightmares and those sleepless
nights.

00:05:23.270 --> 00:05:28.270
But it also fueled something else: his imagination,
and imagination isn't always kind.

00:05:28.270 --> 00:05:32.340
Still, given that he started "writing" his
first work at the tender age of six — he

00:05:32.340 --> 00:05:36.760
dictated a tale called "A History of Moses"
to his mother — it's safe to say that from

00:05:36.760 --> 00:05:40.470
a very young age, he was enthralled by the
power of the story.

00:05:40.470 --> 00:05:44.410
His caretaker must receive at least some of
the credit for this

00:05:44.410 --> 00:05:49.450
It's been suggested, too, that Stevenson’s
illness didn't just inspire him, but magnified

00:05:49.450 --> 00:05:50.860
his the ability to write.

00:05:50.860 --> 00:05:54.930
As he grew older and more established, his
letters began to mention the phenomenon of

00:05:54.930 --> 00:05:58.260
splitting into what he called "myself" and
"the other fellow."

00:05:58.260 --> 00:06:03.540
It was, he said, a side effect of the high
fevers he so frequently suffered from, and

00:06:03.540 --> 00:06:07.200
it'll sound familiar to anyone who's read
the tale of Jekyll and Hyde.

00:06:07.200 --> 00:06:11.290
"Myself," he said, was his rational mind,
while "that other fellow" was the side of

00:06:11.290 --> 00:06:13.760
him that held the darkness and the creativity.

00:06:13.760 --> 00:06:16.210
For Stevenson, that wasn't scary.

00:06:16.210 --> 00:06:20.550
He loved it, it drove him, and he believed
it helped him create the works we all still

00:06:20.550 --> 00:06:21.550
love today.

00:06:21.550 --> 00:06:22.550
Life as a writer?

00:06:22.550 --> 00:06:27.830
You'd better have a back-up plan

00:06:27.830 --> 00:06:32.240
With the benefit of hindsight, it seems like
a given that Stevenson was a natural writer.

00:06:32.240 --> 00:06:36.930
But that belief wasn't always there, and his
parents certainly didn't want their only son

00:06:36.930 --> 00:06:41.060
throwing his life away on what is often a
gamble of a career choice.

00:06:41.060 --> 00:06:45.050
By the time he was a teenager, though, Stevenson
was confident that his future career path

00:06:45.050 --> 00:06:46.920
would involve the written word.

00:06:46.920 --> 00:06:50.940
His father, on the other hand, hoped he would
become an engineer -- the third such professional

00:06:50.940 --> 00:06:52.330
in the family.

00:06:52.330 --> 00:06:55.800
Resigned, Stevenson enrolled in the University
of Edinburgh to study engineering.

00:06:55.800 --> 00:06:59.560
It was quickly clear that going to university
just wasn't for him, and instead of spending

00:06:59.560 --> 00:07:04.610
his time attending lectures and pouring over
his studies, he was more interested in making

00:07:04.610 --> 00:07:05.610
friends.

00:07:05.610 --> 00:07:08.440
And here is where one of those strange coincidences
happen.

00:07:08.440 --> 00:07:13.080
One day, as the story goes, Stevenson was
simply walking down a street in Edinburgh

00:07:13.080 --> 00:07:14.810
when he bumped into another student.

00:07:14.810 --> 00:07:18.639
They got to chatting and ended up heading
to the tavern for a few hours instead of going

00:07:18.639 --> 00:07:19.690
to class.

00:07:19.690 --> 00:07:23.630
The other student was JM Barrie, who would
go on to write Peter Pan.

00:07:23.630 --> 00:07:28.840
Beyond his adventures in Neverland, Barrie
wrote of Stevenson that "Some men of letters,

00:07:28.840 --> 00:07:34.060
not necessarily the greatest, have an indescribable
charm to which we give our hearts."

00:07:34.060 --> 00:07:38.820
A backhanded compliment, maybe, but other
writers who met Stevenson at the same time

00:07:38.820 --> 00:07:43.030
and over the course of his career were completely
taken with him.

00:07:43.030 --> 00:07:47.430
Edmund Gosse would later describe their first
meeting in London like this: "As twilight

00:07:47.430 --> 00:07:52.680
came on, I tore myself away, but Stevenson
walked with me across Hyde Park, and nearly

00:07:52.680 --> 00:07:53.680
to my house.

00:07:53.680 --> 00:07:58.070
He had an engagement, and so had I, but I
walked a mile or two back with him.

00:07:58.070 --> 00:08:02.370
The fountains of talk had been unsealed, and
they drowned the conventions.

00:08:02.370 --> 00:08:05.460
I came home dazzled with my new friend."

00:08:05.460 --> 00:08:09.680
One of the things that constantly struck those
who met him was the contradictory duality

00:08:09.680 --> 00:08:10.930
present with him.

00:08:10.930 --> 00:08:12.810
Outwardly, he was incredibly frail.

00:08:12.810 --> 00:08:17.230
At 5-foot-10, he weighed only 116 pounds when
he was 23 years old.

00:08:17.230 --> 00:08:20.520
When he spoke, though, he could easily capture
the attention of a room.

00:08:20.520 --> 00:08:25.550
So it wasn’t entirely surprising when, in
1871, he did what many people would be terrified

00:08:25.550 --> 00:08:30.210
to do: he told his parents that he wasn't
going to follow in the family footsteps after

00:08:30.210 --> 00:08:31.210
all.

00:08:31.210 --> 00:08:33.490
Stevenson was going to be a writer.

00:08:33.490 --> 00:08:37.019
Anyone who has ever dropped a bombshell like
that on their parents can probably imagine

00:08:37.019 --> 00:08:41.099
how well that went over, but Stevenson’s
parents weren't completely opposed to the

00:08:41.099 --> 00:08:42.099
idea.

00:08:42.099 --> 00:08:46.809
They only insisted that Stevenson have a back-up
plan, so his parents asked him to study law,

00:08:46.809 --> 00:08:50.329
just in case the writing thing didn't take
off like he thought it would.

00:08:50.329 --> 00:09:48.889
He agreed, completing his law degree

00:09:48.889 --> 00:09:50.309
in 1875.

00:09:50.309 --> 00:10:02.410
Love comes when you least expect it

00:10:02.410 --> 00:10:24.350
It 
was just a

00:10:24.350 --> 00:10:28.920
year after graduating from law school that
Stevenson's life would drastically change.

00:10:28.920 --> 00:10:32.829
It started with his want for adventure; imagination
was wonderful, after all, but he wanted to

00:10:32.829 --> 00:10:35.939
experience all the things he had dreamed about
as a child.

00:10:35.939 --> 00:10:40.269
He first planned a canoe trip, beginning in
Antwerp and ending in the north of France.

00:10:40.269 --> 00:10:44.429
Afterward, he headed off to a small town south
of Paris, called Grez-sur-Loing.

00:10:44.429 --> 00:10:49.110
It wasn't a random choice: the town was a
favorite among the artistic and bohemian communities,

00:10:49.110 --> 00:10:53.059
and Stevenson already had a cousin living
there, Robert Alan Mowbray.

00:10:53.059 --> 00:10:56.730
But it was also here that he would meet the
love of his life.

00:10:56.730 --> 00:11:01.269
Fanny Van De Grift Osbourne ended up in France
after a fairly epic story of her own.

00:11:01.269 --> 00:11:05.220
When she was only 17 years old, she married
an American military officer named Samuel

00:11:05.220 --> 00:11:06.220
Osbourne.

00:11:06.220 --> 00:11:10.139
Following the American Civil War, he headed
off to seek his fortune in the silver mines

00:11:10.139 --> 00:11:11.139
of Nevada.

00:11:11.139 --> 00:11:13.820
He told his family his wife and daughter to
join him, and they did.

00:11:13.820 --> 00:11:15.089
Fanny didn't like it the West at all.

00:11:15.089 --> 00:11:19.589
The Indiana native bore two more children,
but her husband proved less than honorable.

00:11:19.589 --> 00:11:24.200
Sick of his cheating and his prolonged, mysterious
disappearances, she packed up her children

00:11:24.200 --> 00:11:25.730
and left Nevada for Belgium.

00:11:25.730 --> 00:11:27.649
This, too, was 1875.

00:11:27.649 --> 00:11:31.610
It was a time when most women wouldn't have
even considered such a thing, and the first

00:11:31.610 --> 00:11:35.449
obstacle they faced showed just how different
times were.

00:11:35.449 --> 00:11:39.700
She and her daughter had planned on studying
art in Antwerp, but were told that women were

00:11:39.700 --> 00:11:41.399
not allowed to enroll in art schools.

00:11:41.399 --> 00:11:45.930
They moved on to Paris, but when her youngest
son took ill and died, she retired to a small

00:11:45.930 --> 00:11:48.490
town in the countryside with her remaining
children.

00:11:48.490 --> 00:11:53.480
The town was, of course, the small artists'
community of Grez-sur-Loing, and that's where

00:11:53.480 --> 00:11:54.480
she met Stevenson.

00:11:54.480 --> 00:11:56.110
They had a lot working against them.

00:11:56.110 --> 00:12:00.170
She was 11 years older than him; she had two
children already; technically, she was still

00:12:00.170 --> 00:12:01.449
married to Osbourne.

00:12:01.449 --> 00:12:05.670
Stevenson, meanwhile, was fresh out of law
school, had no job or career to speak of,

00:12:05.670 --> 00:12:09.249
and was still trying to figure out just how
he was going to turn this whole writing thing

00:12:09.249 --> 00:12:10.679
into a livelihood.

00:12:10.679 --> 00:12:15.490
He had already written one book based on his
earlier canoe trip, but one book won't support

00:12:15.490 --> 00:12:16.490
a family.

00:12:16.490 --> 00:12:18.070
Stevenson courted Fanny for two years.

00:12:18.070 --> 00:12:21.600
They enjoyed each other’s company, but the
presence of another man in her life eventually

00:12:21.600 --> 00:12:24.620
forced Fanny to reconsider the one she’d
left behind.

00:12:24.620 --> 00:12:28.600
She decided to return to California to give
her husband another chance.

00:12:28.600 --> 00:12:32.529
Abandoned by the woman he loved, Stevenson
set out on another adventure — one that

00:12:32.529 --> 00:12:37.689
would become the inspiration for his second
book, Travels with a Donkey in the Cevennes.

00:12:37.689 --> 00:12:42.279
For 12 days, he hiked the Cevennes with the
help of a donkey who carried his baggage for

00:12:42.279 --> 00:12:47.259
him; he covered 120 miles of difficult country,
which wasn't just ambitious -- it was outright

00:12:47.259 --> 00:12:48.259
dangerous.

00:12:48.259 --> 00:12:52.999
Not everyone was a fan of the book, particularly
because of the treatment of its titular character.

00:12:52.999 --> 00:12:57.300
When Stevenson bought his donkey, he named
her Modestine, and by the time the 12 days

00:12:57.300 --> 00:13:00.319
were over, her value had been cut in half.

00:13:00.319 --> 00:13:04.389
Getting her to move along involved Stevenson
delivering regular blows, and one critic summed

00:13:04.389 --> 00:13:09.809
up just how horrible it was, writing, "Raw
legs and bleeding skin do not move him in

00:13:09.809 --> 00:13:10.809
the least."

00:13:10.809 --> 00:13:15.239
The trip had been taken at least in part in
an attempt to heal a broken heart, but their

00:13:15.239 --> 00:13:16.879
story wasn't over yet.

00:13:16.879 --> 00:13:19.809
In 1879, Stevenson received a telegram from
California.

00:13:19.809 --> 00:13:23.470
Fanny was sick, her husband hadn't changed,
and she regretted ever leaving Europe.

00:13:23.470 --> 00:13:27.989
In a grand gesture that really is the stuff
of a classic romance novel, Stevenson hopped

00:13:27.989 --> 00:13:30.980
on a ship in Glasgow and sailed for America.

00:13:30.980 --> 00:13:34.870
After landing in New York, he took a train
all the way from the east coast to the west,

00:13:34.870 --> 00:13:39.829
and by the time he reached her in Monterey,
California, the journey had taken a terrifying

00:13:39.829 --> 00:13:42.339
toll on his already fragile health.

00:13:42.339 --> 00:13:46.420
But for Fanny and her children, it seemed
worth every sacrifice.

00:13:46.420 --> 00:13:50.049
By December of that 1879, she had formally
divorced Samuel Osbourne.

00:13:50.049 --> 00:13:52.869
Less than six months later, she married Stevenson.

00:13:52.869 --> 00:13:56.509
Those early months were filled with extreme
highs and lows.

00:13:56.509 --> 00:14:01.079
Stevenson and his new bride stayed in Napa
Valley for a time, where he wrote The Silverado

00:14:01.079 --> 00:14:02.079
Squatters.

00:14:02.079 --> 00:14:07.049
But it was also there that he became seriously
ill, as he developed a new symptom: he began

00:14:07.049 --> 00:14:08.069
to cough up blood.

00:14:08.069 --> 00:14:14.839
Was it a death sentence… or did it make
life worth living?

00:14:14.839 --> 00:14:19.579
Stevenson’s poor health never really rebounded
after the long trip from Scotland to California,

00:14:19.579 --> 00:14:23.360
and for the remainder of his life, the little
family moved several times in an attempt to

00:14:23.360 --> 00:14:26.470
find a climate more agreeable to his delicate
constitution.

00:14:26.470 --> 00:14:30.529
The first stop was England, where they lived
until 1887 — save for the winters, which

00:14:30.529 --> 00:14:32.589
were spent in the south of France.

00:14:32.589 --> 00:14:36.509
Stevenson supported his family by writing
essays and bits of travel journalism, providing

00:14:36.509 --> 00:14:41.449
readers with his thoughts on dogs, umbrellas,
and a litany of other common observations.

00:14:41.449 --> 00:14:46.429
However, this time is more associated with
the publication of his major career highlights:

00:14:46.429 --> 00:14:50.899
he wrote and published Treasure Island in
1883, plus The Strange Case of Dr. Jekyll

00:14:50.899 --> 00:14:54.449
and Mr. Hyde, and Kidnapped, both in 1886.

00:14:54.449 --> 00:14:57.639
Treasure Island, in particular, came from
a brilliant moment of inspiration.

00:14:57.639 --> 00:15:02.360
The family was away on holiday, staying in
a small Scottish town, and the day was rainy

00:15:02.360 --> 00:15:03.360
and dreary.

00:15:03.360 --> 00:15:08.649
The story varies a bit — some say Stevenson
was the one to first draw the map of a mysterious

00:15:08.649 --> 00:15:12.350
island, while his stepson, Lloyd, would later
claim it was he who drew it.

00:15:12.350 --> 00:15:17.170
Either way, a map was drawn, and Stevenson
began to name points of interest: Spyglass

00:15:17.170 --> 00:15:22.119
Hill, Skeleton Island… then, someone added
three red crosses and a title of Treasure

00:15:22.119 --> 00:15:27.999
Island, and the foundation of one of the literary
world's most beloved pirate tales was formed.

00:15:27.999 --> 00:15:32.269
And here's a little aside — the map included
in various editions of Treasure Island was

00:15:32.269 --> 00:15:33.389
not the original.

00:15:33.389 --> 00:15:37.110
That original map was lost, somewhere between
submitting it to the publisher and getting

00:15:37.110 --> 00:15:38.339
the book printed.

00:15:38.339 --> 00:15:42.889
While Stevenson did redraw the map himself,
he was reportedly never quite happy with it…

00:15:42.889 --> 00:15:48.869
and the fact that the original map for Treasure
Island is actually lost is pretty darn appropriate.

00:15:48.869 --> 00:15:53.249
In 1887, Stevenson’s father died; with no
real reason to stay in Britain any longer,

00:15:53.249 --> 00:15:57.290
he and his family left — first for a year
in America, then to the South Pacific.

00:15:57.290 --> 00:16:01.739
Eventually, they reached Samoa, where they
decided to stay, at least for a moment.

00:16:01.739 --> 00:16:06.079
In Samoa, he not only wrote a lot — often
from the confines of a sickbed — but he

00:16:06.079 --> 00:16:08.309
also became a treasured member of the community.

00:16:08.309 --> 00:16:12.489
He immersed himself in local politics and
activism, and was even given the title of

00:16:12.489 --> 00:16:15.559
Tusitala, or the teller of tales.

00:16:15.559 --> 00:16:19.959
Originally, Samoa had just been another stop
on the itinerary, with no real intention of

00:16:19.959 --> 00:16:24.170
staying there, but Stevenson gradually came
to the conclusion that the climate would be

00:16:24.170 --> 00:16:25.329
good for his health.

00:16:25.329 --> 00:16:29.490
With his mind made up, Stevenson bought a
300-acre estate and settled in to the place

00:16:29.490 --> 00:16:32.690
that Steveson and Fanny considered to be their
first real home.

00:16:32.690 --> 00:16:36.459
Sadly, it would also be their last.

00:16:36.459 --> 00:16:41.750
Samoa’s Setting Sun

00:16:41.750 --> 00:16:44.649
On the surface, their lives should have been
idyllic.

00:16:44.649 --> 00:16:48.179
Locals and travellers — particularly those
who had made the journey to Samoa from Scotland

00:16:48.179 --> 00:16:50.110
— were always welcome at the estate.

00:16:50.110 --> 00:16:54.370
They celebrated everything from birthdays
to American Thanksgiving; food and songs were

00:16:54.370 --> 00:16:55.859
more plentiful than the guests.

00:16:55.859 --> 00:16:59.769
They had around 15 servants working to keep
the estate running, and Stevenson himself

00:16:59.769 --> 00:17:01.529
found solace in working the land.

00:17:01.529 --> 00:17:06.730
He took to gardening and weeding, in particular,
once writing, "Farming is amusing; literature,

00:17:06.730 --> 00:17:09.870
save in results, is intolerably stupid.

00:17:09.870 --> 00:17:12.799
[...] Nothing is so interesting as weeding."

00:17:12.799 --> 00:17:15.490
There was an unhappy undercurrent, though.

00:17:15.490 --> 00:17:19.610
Fanny aspired to be an artist of her own,
and when he told her that she had "the soul

00:17:19.610 --> 00:17:22.179
of a peasant," she was extraordinarily offended.

00:17:22.179 --> 00:17:27.360
Stevenson had meant it as a compliment — watching
gardeners and botanists work to plant and

00:17:27.360 --> 00:17:32.500
cultivate the land had made him wonder if
writing was real work; physical labor, it

00:17:32.500 --> 00:17:34.960
seemed to him, was a much more worthy endeavor.

00:17:34.960 --> 00:17:39.269
But Fanny didn't take it that way, and the
artistic anxiety she had always struggled

00:17:39.269 --> 00:17:41.440
with only got worse.

00:17:41.440 --> 00:17:45.139
Fanny's very presence in Stevenson's life
continued to be controversial.

00:17:45.139 --> 00:17:49.120
While Stevenson's parents had come to accept
her — his widowed mother lived with them

00:17:49.120 --> 00:17:51.519
in Samoa — his friends did not.

00:17:51.519 --> 00:17:55.010
They resented her for telling friends they
couldn't visit, and for inserting herself

00:17:55.010 --> 00:17:56.019
in his work.

00:17:56.019 --> 00:18:00.360
She long claimed that she was responsible
for the success of Jekyll and Hyde, claiming

00:18:00.360 --> 00:18:04.490
that she'd insisted he completely rewrite
the first draft of the work.

00:18:04.490 --> 00:18:08.470
While there was no love lost between Fanny
and Stevenson's friends, there's little doubt

00:18:08.470 --> 00:18:09.820
that they truly loved each other.

00:18:09.820 --> 00:18:14.919
But as the years at the estate in Samoa went
on, Fanny continued to struggle more and more

00:18:14.919 --> 00:18:17.840
with a nervous illness that had plagued her
for years.

00:18:17.840 --> 00:18:22.430
As for Stevenson, he did continue to write
while living at the island estate, but he

00:18:22.430 --> 00:18:25.220
would never match the success of his earlier
work.

00:18:25.220 --> 00:18:29.640
Most of his later publications were historical
works or travel essays, and when they didn’t

00:18:29.640 --> 00:18:32.510
take off, he grew increasingly worried about
money.

00:18:32.510 --> 00:18:36.190
That stress ratched up with the release of
one of his histories that detailed the fight

00:18:36.190 --> 00:18:40.590
between colonial powers that were vying for
control of the island; when the book was published,

00:18:40.590 --> 00:18:44.090
he began to worry that it was so controversial
that he could be deported.

00:18:44.090 --> 00:18:49.419
Stevenson’s stress and health issues finally
hit critical mass on December 3, 1894.

00:18:49.419 --> 00:18:53.149
He worked on a fiction project in the morning
and wrote some letters in the afternoon.

00:18:53.149 --> 00:18:55.659
Later, he helped Fanny make some mayonnaise.

00:18:55.659 --> 00:18:59.100
That's what he was doing when he collapsed
-- he was dead by the evening, likely of a

00:18:59.100 --> 00:19:00.410
cerebral hemorrhage.

00:19:00.410 --> 00:19:04.789
Robert Louis Stevenson was buried on the island,
and his grave is marked by a stone on which

00:19:04.789 --> 00:19:07.100
is inscribed the epitaph he wrote for himself:

00:19:07.100 --> 00:19:11.380
Under the wide and starry sky
Dig the grave and let me lie

00:19:11.380 --> 00:19:15.889
Glad I did life and gladly die
And I laid me down with a will

00:19:15.889 --> 00:19:20.600
This be the verse you grave for me
Here he lies where he longed to be

00:19:20.600 --> 00:19:25.539
Home is the sailor home from the sea
And the hunter home from the hill

00:19:25.539 --> 00:19:29.770
Fanny and her children only stayed on the
estate for a few months after his death, although

00:19:29.770 --> 00:19:33.929
her ashes were buried beside him after her
passing in 1914.

00:19:33.929 --> 00:19:37.929
When they were reunited, it was to a huge
celebration on Samoa, as they were laid to

00:19:37.929 --> 00:19:42.580
rest in what has been called "the most inaccessible
grave in English letters."

00:19:42.580 --> 00:19:47.210
It’s an appropriate description for the
final resting place of the man who wrote the

00:19:47.210 --> 00:20:02.779
world's most famous, beloved tale of pirates
and buried treasure.

